From e4e9bc45ec27eefbe43b5efd3dadf04ea9783013 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 30 Jul 2026 11:19:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] css: resolve the system colours and the logical viewport
 units
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two whole-canvas WPT failures from issue #1491 come from <color>/<length>
keywords that never resolved.

System colours (problem 28): CssSystemColors carried only Field/FieldText,
so Canvas, CanvasText, ButtonFace and the rest fell through to the
named-colour lookup — which does not know them — and resolved to black.
forced-colors-mode-20.html paints body { background-color: Canvas } and
rendered a 98% black canvas against Chromium's 98% white; the whole
system-colour family failed the same way. Fill in the CSS Color 4 §6 table
from the light palette (what the Chromium references are screenshots of),
map the §6.2 deprecated keywords onto their aliases, and add a
CssColorScheme overload so a dark used colour scheme can select the dark
palette. forced-colors still computes to none — nothing emulates it.

Logical viewport units (problem 30): vb and vi did not parse at all, so
page-box-008-print.html's block-size: 100vb box got no size and the test
painted the body's hotpink background where Chromium paints a yellow box.
Add vb/vi resolved against the root element's writing mode (CSS Values 4
§6.1.4 defines them on the root's axes, so a per-pass factor is the right
granularity), plus the small/large/dynamic variants — sv*/lv*/dv* coincide
with the default viewport in a headless render with no retractable UA
chrome, so they canonicalise onto it.

Canonicalising means the written unit can be longer than the unit it
reports (svmin -> vmin), which broke every site that split number-from-unit
by the canonical length: '100svmin' yielded the number '100s'. GetUnit now
also reports the length as written, and the three trimming sites use it.
